WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.946
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.447 --> 00:00:14.239
FELD: Good morning.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.532
Did everybody have a good
breakfast this morning?

00:00:22.532 --> 00:00:24.110
[INAUDIBLE]

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:24.610
Okay.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.400
I have two announcements first.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.770
First of all, we have a
handout on the physics

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:36.085
of karate, which is going to be
the subject of today's lecture.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.570
Would everybody
please settle down?

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:45.290
The second thing
is that many people

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.350
have come to Professor Redwine
and myself with the freshman

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.330
evaluation forms.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:53.600
These forms should be brought
to your recitation section.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.650
They should not
be brought to us.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:02.000
So you hand them in to
your recitation instructor.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.810
So today, we have a
special discussion

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:14.180
of some applications of Newton's
laws to the physics of karate--

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.560
to karate.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.410
And I hope everybody--
will everybody

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.600
please sit down quickly
and take your seats?

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.760
This will be-- everybody
get settled, please.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:41.660
So karate is an art, a sport,
and it requires demonstrations.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:46.490
And so I've asked a couple of
colleagues to join me here.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.480
And I would like to
introduce my colleagues now.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.310
Come on out.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.610
Sir--

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:05.940
This is David Feld, Alexandra
Feld, and Jonathan Feld--

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.510
just a coincidence.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:17.170
Now, karate is something
which everybody knows about.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:23.190
One knows about the oriental
connections with it.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.800
People have seen
these demonstrations

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.880
in which fabulous piles of
bricks and blocks of concrete

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.630
are demolished with a hand.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:38.320
And there's a lot of physics
involved in that process.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.190
After all, how is it
that a piece of bone

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.760
can break a piece of
concrete and not the reverse?

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.390
It has to do with Newton's laws.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:49.140
It has to do with
our understanding

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.060
of various concepts of
physics and mechanics.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.710
And one of the
extraordinary things

00:02:55.710 --> 00:03:01.380
is that the ancient oriental
masters who studied karate

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:05.850
as far as 2,000 years ago knew
these secrets, even though they

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.520
didn't know Newton's laws.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:13.230
And I could tell you that if
you study the motions which

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.030
have been developed in
karate, if you study

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.540
the various techniques
which have been developed,

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:23.310
you find that they really use
Newton's laws and the laws

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.230
of mechanics in
very special ways

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:31.040
to make nearly optimal
use of the human body.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.360
Now may I have the
first slide, please?

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.630
I guess I can
control these myself.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.270
I tried to have some slides
today, because there's

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:41.540
a lot of material to present.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.120
And I want to be sure
I have time to do that.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:51.690
First, I want to say that
this lecture is dedicated

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:57.000
to the memory of Ron McNair,
our karate instructor

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.860
and a graduate of MIT.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.720
May I have the
next slide, please?

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:03.856
Oh, I guess I can
do this myself.

00:04:03.856 --> 00:04:05.750
Forward.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:11.540
Now the knowledge of
karate and the knowledge

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.310
of the Asian fighting
arts was actually

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:15.000
also known in the West.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.610
And here's a picture
from the Vatican

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:24.640
Museum of two Greek wrestlers
Kruegas and Damoxenos

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.965
And this was a story
which goes back to 400 BC.

00:04:28.965 --> 00:04:32.220
And actually, according
to the legend,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.440
Kruegas and Damoxenos
were two great fighters

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.440
who wrestled with one another.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.480
And they wrestled
all day and all night

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.340
to see who would be the best.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.260
And neither could
beat the other.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.360
And so finally, they
decided that they

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.760
would have a contest
with each delivering

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.720
one blow to the other.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.500
So first, Kruegas elected
to give a very hard blow

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:01.390
to Damoxenos' head, which
he managed to withstand.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:08.120
And then Damoxenos then asked
Kruegas to raise his arm.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.540
And you can see here
the point of this story

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.630
is the very fine
spear-hand strike, which

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:15.250
was then delivered.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.060
And without going into the
anatomical problems which

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:25.720
followed, suffice to say that
Kruegas came out the victor.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.870
I will eliminate some of the
more gory aspects of the story.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.260
Now let's see here.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.230
Next slide, please.

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:42.243
I don't seem to be
able to control this.

00:05:42.243 --> 00:05:43.660
That's all for the
slides for now.

00:05:47.465 --> 00:05:52.330
Now we want to tell you
about the physics of karate.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:57.225
And we want to tell you a
little bit about karate itself.

00:05:57.225 --> 00:05:58.600
And we want to
tell you something

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:03.820
about the philosophy
of karate, also.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:08.170
As I mentioned, karate
is an Asian oriental art.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.420
It's practiced in all the
different countries of Asia.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:13.330
Its roots go back
2,000 years or more.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:18.940
And modern karate-- the word
"karate" means "empty hand"--

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:20.530
was a form of
weaponless fighting

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:23.290
which was developed in
the island of Okinawa

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:27.320
in the 17th century, when the
Japanese took over that island

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:32.080
and prevented the soldiers
and people of Okinawa

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.040
from practicing
their feudal combats.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:38.020
And so they developed
this technique

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.880
of fighting without weapons.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.650
It then was transferred to
Japan in the early 1900s,

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:48.910
and has become
very popular today.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:53.110
Karate is a purely defensive
sport and a defensive art.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.930
It is not something which
is developed so that you

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:58.210
go out and beat people up.

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:00.130
And as you will see, I
will try to emphasize

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:02.100
in various points
of this lecture

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.680
the very defensive
nature of karate.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.750
And I also want to say
that karate is also

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:14.050
very important for developing
mental concentration, as well

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.700
as physical strength.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.440
And the mental
discipline in karate

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:21.612
is at least as important as
the physical aspects of it.

00:07:21.612 --> 00:07:23.320
So what we're going
to do in this lecture

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.293
is I want to show you some
of the basic techniques.

00:07:26.293 --> 00:07:28.460
I then want to show you
something very interesting--

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:31.890
the central part of karate
which are called Kata.

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:37.270
They're very beautiful, little
dances, kinds of dances.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:38.560
We'll get into that.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.320
Then we'll discuss the theory
and experimental aspects

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:44.360
of breaking and
some other things.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.080
So let us begin now
by demonstrating

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:49.570
some of the basic techniques
with David and Jonathan.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:56.150
So now--

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:58.400
FELD: Rei.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.810
FELD: Now the
elements of karate,

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.610
just like the
elements of physics,

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.250
are very well defined
and very well focused.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:10.030
And there are many, many basic
techniques, 70 or 80 of them.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:11.770
Hand techniques,
foot techniques,

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.703
head techniques--
any part of the body

00:08:13.703 --> 00:08:15.370
can be used as a
weapon, but it's always

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.080
used in a very special way.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:19.690
The most basic
technique in karate

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:22.060
is the front forward
punch, the Seiken Zuki.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.240
Now, this really
illustrates many things

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.370
about a typical
karate technique.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.409
The first thing is
that karate is totally

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:34.000
different from Western sports.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.240
You know how we have
all this follow through,

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:39.970
and these wide swings,
and so forth, and so on.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:42.190
Karate is completely
the opposite.

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:45.520
The ancient masters discovered
that the most important concept

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.140
was focus.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:50.410
In each karate technique, the
idea is to develop the largest

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:53.860
amount of velocity, the
largest amount of force,

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.480
and the largest
amount of momentum--

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.820
something that we just started
talking about in the last

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:58.960
lecture--

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.090
to be able as
quickly as possible

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:04.090
to deliver a high amount
of kinetic energy,

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:06.058
and then to transfer
it to the target.

00:09:06.058 --> 00:09:07.850
Okay, let's demonstrate
that at full speed.

00:09:10.622 --> 00:09:13.493
FELD: [SHOUTS]

00:09:13.493 --> 00:09:14.160
FELD: All right.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:17.580
Now, not only are
there karate strikes,

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.040
but there are also blocks
for each technique.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.130
And the blocks are done
in a very economical way.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:25.848
So now let us demonstrate how
this kind of a Seiken Zuki--

00:09:25.848 --> 00:09:27.640
oh, yeah, do you want
to do it to the face?

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:28.420
Demonstrate to the face.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:28.920
Go ahead.

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:34.470
FELD: [SHOUTS]

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.780
FELD: I should also mention
that black belts like this

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.070
are very precise in
their techniques.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:42.840
And it's expected that when
demonstrations like this occur,

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:47.250
that there is just paper-thin
precision in all of the moves.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.380
So let's demonstrate
some blocks and counters.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.510
This is going to be some
sort of an upper block.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:52.872
Go ahead.

00:09:52.872 --> 00:09:56.802
FELD:
[VOCALIZING WITH EACH PUNCH]

00:09:56.802 --> 00:09:58.510
FELD: So notice, here's
a typical example

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:02.350
where instead of meeting
force with force head on,

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:05.620
what happens is a certain
amount of transverse force

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:08.020
and transverse
momentum is delivered

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:10.630
to simply redirect the
technique and render

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:12.910
it harmless to the opponent.

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:15.850
Now Jonathan is going to
demonstrate another technique,

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:16.990
a hammer fist.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:19.450
This is a technique which is
very effective in breaking

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.040
piles of concrete.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.200
And David will show a
typical block for that.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.120
Go ahead.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:30.490
FELD:
[VOCALIZING WITH EACH PUNCH]

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:32.620
FELD: Now, there are
many, many other kinds

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:34.120
of karate techniques.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:36.520
And I want to emphasize that
all of the parts of the body

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:37.750
are used in these techniques.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:39.820
Let us just show you
one-- no, no, one kick.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.580
This is a typical karate kick.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:48.340
It's called the
sidekick, the Yoko Gari.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:49.240
Go ahead.

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:50.920
Now you can see
here, it's delivered

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:53.540
with the knife part of the--

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:56.660
hello-- it's delivered with
the knife part of the foot

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.910
to minimize the contact area.

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.700
And the toes are
curled to prevent them

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:03.890
from being broken
off in the technique.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.270
And there's a very large
amount of energy and power

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.370
which can be delivered
in this technique.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:09.508
Go ahead.

00:11:09.508 --> 00:11:12.490
FELD: [VOCALIZING]

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:14.810
FELD: Thank you very much.

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:15.970
Now let me show--

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:21.470
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:24.850
Now I'd like to show some slides
of some physical measurements

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:28.420
that we've done over the
years to be able to study

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:30.280
some of these things.

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:35.080
And this is actually a strobe
movie that we made where each

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.770
of these techniques--
this is a Seiken Zuki--

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:41.410
and each of these techniques
was taken one millisecond apart.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.140
And then by just
studying the position,

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:49.340
we could study the velocity
as a function of time.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.890
And you can see that in
this particular technique,

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:54.410
for example, the velocity rose.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:55.360
Here's the focus.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.400
Remember, I told you
it is very focused.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.990
The velocity is
maximum at this point.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:02.740
And when the distance
got to be about 85%

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:05.710
of the way to the
target, the velocity

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:09.190
was around 7 and 1/2
meters per second.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:15.010
And the mass of a
fist is typically

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:17.260
of the order of 1
kilogram, so that

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:23.440
means 1/2 mV squared would
be 1/2 times 1 times 100,

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:25.300
or say 50 joules.

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:28.510
So 50 joules of energy
is being delivered

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:32.170
in a time of maybe a tenth of
a second or a few hundredths

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:33.100
a second.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.490
So that's maybe 500
watts of power, which is

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:37.872
an interesting amount of power.

00:12:37.872 --> 00:12:40.330
It doesn't sound like a lot of
power, but on the other hand

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:43.360
it's certainly enough to
break huge pieces of concrete.

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:46.460
Next slide, please.

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:47.950
I can't control this.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:50.038
This is a hammer
fist-- the same thing.

00:12:50.038 --> 00:12:52.580
Here, we see velocities of the
order of 10 meters per second.

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:54.490
Next, please.

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:56.320
These are, again, strobe movies.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:00.520
And here's just an example
of an expert karate expert.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:03.100
Notice with a kick,
you get of the order

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:07.050
of 10 to 15 meters per second,
with various kinds of strikes,

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:09.950
10 to 15 meters per second,
and so forth, and so on.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:12.820
So that gives us now a
good piece of information

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:17.110
as to how much speed and how
much energy can be delivered

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:18.770
in a karate technique.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:21.820
Next slide, please.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:23.070
Good, good, shut them off now.

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:24.570
Thank you.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.370
Now the next thing
is I would like

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:28.860
to get right into
this most central

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.290
and to me, the most
interesting aspect of karate--

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:32.700
the kata.

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:36.280
And these kata are very,
very wonderful things.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.790
They are very much
like classical ballet.

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:41.700
They are prescribed
sets of movements

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:49.740
in which the karate practitioner
fights an imaginary series

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:50.880
of opponents.

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:53.340
And there are only a
limited number of kata.

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:56.040
For example, in
the Shotokan style

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.040
of karate, which is the one that
we'll be demonstrating today,

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.020
there are about 50 kata.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:04.180
And each one of them has to
be done in a very special way.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:06.690
There's the same
kind of perfection.

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:09.600
It's very, very closely
related to ballet, I find.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:14.160
And in doing these, you're
really never competing

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:16.050
with anyone except yourself.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:17.970
And one can spend
one's whole lifetime

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.700
just constantly trying
to perfect the kata,

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:22.640
make them more and
more beautiful.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:24.390
The other thing I want
to say about karate

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:29.640
is that it's a sport in which
age, sex, and weight have

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:31.650
no meaning at all,
so that the tiniest

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:37.440
person can be just as effective
as a big hockey person.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:40.590
So now let us
demonstrate some kata.

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:43.560
And first, I would
like to ask Alexandra

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:48.780
