[VOCALIZING WITH MOVEMENTS]

00:21:02.467 --> 00:21:05.449
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:14.840
I forgot my notes.

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:19.550
Now we're going
to do [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:22.520
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:33.390
You have a handout, which
describes a lot of information

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:35.210
about the physics of breaking.

00:21:35.210 --> 00:21:39.300
And I'd like to present a
simplified discussion here.

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:41.850
There's a lot of interesting
physics which goes on here.

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:43.310
May I have the
first slide, please.

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:51.110
Can I have the
first slide, please.

00:21:51.110 --> 00:21:53.640
So the question we want to
address ourselves to now

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:57.470
is, we have a piece
of patio block

00:21:57.470 --> 00:22:01.610
like this one, which
weighs 6 and 1/2 kilograms.

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:04.910
How is it that a
fist can actually

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:10.320
break a huge block like this,
let alone a pile of them?

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.750
And we can also be sure what
happens to the case of a board.

00:22:17.690 --> 00:22:21.230
These are called 12-inch
white pine boards.

00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:23.055
And they're used
for building houses.

00:22:25.873 --> 00:22:27.290
Maybe I should get
the microphone.

00:22:27.290 --> 00:22:28.360
Here.

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:30.550
Is this better?

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:31.260
Is this working?

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:31.760
No?

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:41.730
So let's begin by
having a simple model,

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:43.375
a [INAUDIBLE] curve.

00:22:43.375 --> 00:22:44.950
May I have the
next slide, please?

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:51.820
[INAUDIBLE] turn
out all the lights.

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:58.270
So let's consider a block which
is the dimensions l by h by w.

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:02.110
This block will be
impacted in the center

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:06.010
by a fist, or a foot, or
some other part of the body,

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:09.580
producing a certain
deflection, d.

00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:12.190
Basically, what
happens is the block

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:14.740
responds elastically, whether
it's a piece of concrete

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:17.320
or a piece of bone
or a piece of wood.

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:22.780
And when the forces, the
stresses inside the material

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:25.750
reach a high enough
point, then what happens

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:28.150
is that breakage occurs.

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:30.440
Rupture occurs.

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:33.460
Now if you think about
it, a simple approximation

00:23:33.460 --> 00:23:39.140
to this situation is that
the block is like a mass,

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:41.970
and the mass has a spring
on it of spring constant k.

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:43.630
You're experts on that.

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:46.760
And actually, the mass is only
half the mass of the block.

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:49.388
And the reason for that
is, roughly speaking,

00:23:49.388 --> 00:23:51.430
that only the middle part
of the block is moving.

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:53.430
The end parts of the block
are simply supported,

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:55.840
as shown in the diagram.

00:23:55.840 --> 00:23:59.780
And the fist is like a body
traveling with the mass, m,

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:02.030
which is in the
order of a kilogram,

00:24:02.030 --> 00:24:07.420
and a velocity v. So now, what
happens in the breaking process

00:24:07.420 --> 00:24:10.630
from this simple point of view
is that initially, the fist

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:14.680
has a certain amount of
kinetic energy, 1/2 mV squared.

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:19.570
And it strikes the
mass-spring system.

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:24.130
And a collision occurs in
which momentum is conserved.

00:24:24.130 --> 00:24:28.125
As you'll see, it's not an
energy conserving collision,

00:24:28.125 --> 00:24:29.875
but it is a momentum
conserving collision.

00:24:29.875 --> 00:24:32.600
And we began to talk
about momentum last time.

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:35.860
And what happens is that at
the end of the collision, when

00:24:35.860 --> 00:24:38.170
the spring has
started to compress,

00:24:38.170 --> 00:24:42.490
the mass and the spring mass
and the [INAUDIBLE] mass are

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:43.810
together.

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:45.510
And a certain
amount of energy has

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:49.290
been coupled into the target,
which is called k e final,

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:51.430
that we'll calculate
in a movement.

00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:55.150
Now, once that final kinetic
energy is in the target,

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:57.430
it gets translated
to the spring,

00:24:57.430 --> 00:25:02.110
turns into elastic
energy, 1/2 k x squared.

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:05.140
And when that amount
of elastic energy

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:09.830
is enough to break the
material, then rupture occurs.

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:13.600
Now, that breakage occurs when
a critical deflection, d0,

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:14.500
occurs.

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:17.960
In a moment, we'll talk about
how you figure that out.

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:19.840
So what I want to
first talk about

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:24.880
is the step of coupling
the energy from the fist

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:25.712
into the mass.

00:25:25.712 --> 00:25:27.420
And it turns out that
it's very important

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:28.795
to be able to
think that the fact

00:25:28.795 --> 00:25:32.690
that it is a very different
physical situation if you

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:35.040
have a piece of
concrete, for example.

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:40.110
Then the concrete-- this one
weighs 6 and 1/2 kilograms.

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:43.240
The fist weighs a
fraction of a kilogram.

00:25:43.240 --> 00:25:45.620
On the other hand, when
we have a piece of board,

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:47.530
the board only weighs
a quarter of a kilogram

00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:49.630
and the fist is much heavier.

00:25:49.630 --> 00:25:53.440
It really affects the coupling
of energy considerably.

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:56.050
So let me just explain to you
right away that kinetic energy

00:25:56.050 --> 00:25:56.830
is not conserved.

00:25:56.830 --> 00:25:59.260
And I'll explain that right now.

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:00.820
Let's use momentum conservation.

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:02.403
You remember, we
talked about the fact

00:26:02.403 --> 00:26:05.560
that the momentum was the
mass times the velocity.

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:09.070
Initially, we have a fist with
momentum mass times velocity.

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:11.590
Capital M is the
mass of the fist.

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:14.200
At the end, after the
collision has occurred,

00:26:14.200 --> 00:26:18.200
we have a total object of
mass m plus M with a velocity

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:21.550
v. So we can easily
solve for little v

00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:24.820
and get v equals
M over M plus m.

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:29.190
Now the final kinetic energy
is then the total mass

00:26:29.190 --> 00:26:31.580
times little v squared over 2.

00:26:31.580 --> 00:26:34.600
And if we substitute this
v, then we immediately

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:37.475
get that the final
kinetic energy is

00:26:37.475 --> 00:26:38.990
equal to the initial
kinetic energy,

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:42.550
but reduced by a
factor M over m plus M.

00:26:42.550 --> 00:26:48.370
So you can see that if little m,
which is the mass of the block,

00:26:48.370 --> 00:26:51.640
is very small, then most of the
kinetic energy is coupled in.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:53.720
That would be the
case of the board.

00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:57.550
But in the case of the concrete,
where little m is huge,

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:01.630
then only a small fraction,
maybe 20% or 15% is coupled in.

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:04.690
And the rest of the energy
goes into the deformation

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:06.915
of the object, namely, the fist.

00:27:06.915 --> 00:27:08.790
And we'll talk about
that a little bit later,

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:10.940
or the part of the body.

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:15.040
So finally, we derive a formula
that the initial energy is

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:20.440
equal to the final energy
times M plus m over M.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:23.260
But then remember, we said that
that final kinetic energy was

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:25.550
going to become elastic energy.

00:27:25.550 --> 00:27:27.220
And so we finally
have this equation,

00:27:27.220 --> 00:27:30.400
that the initial
kinetic energy to be

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:34.180
able to break the object
is equal to this mass ratio

00:27:34.180 --> 00:27:38.470
times the elastic energy
necessary to stress

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:41.200
the board to the point of
fracture, or the brick.

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:42.865
Next slide.

00:27:42.865 --> 00:27:44.740
So the question is, how
can we then calculate

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:47.230
this critical
distance d0, which is

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:50.348
the amount you have to deflect
the body in order to break it?

00:27:50.348 --> 00:27:51.890
Well, now I get to
tell you something

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:54.960
about mechanical engineering.

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:56.223
Here's the block again.

00:27:56.223 --> 00:27:57.640
And there are two
parameters which

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.255
determine the elastic
and breaking properties

00:28:00.255 --> 00:28:01.910
of materials.

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:06.530
One is Young's modulus, which
is a bulk property which

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:09.580
characterizes the
springiness of a material

00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:11.860
without regard to its geometry.

00:28:11.860 --> 00:28:13.870
And then there's some
geometrical factor which

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:15.370
depends upon the shape of it.

00:28:15.370 --> 00:28:17.950
Similarly, Young's
modulus has dimensions

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:20.600
of Newton's per square meter.

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:22.570
This is force per unit area.

00:28:22.570 --> 00:28:25.810
Similarly, sigma 0,
modulus of rupture,

00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:28.205
is an parameter which
describes, again

00:28:28.205 --> 00:28:31.510
in Newtons per square meter,
with a bulk material how

00:28:31.510 --> 00:28:35.480
much stress do you have
to apply to produce

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:39.910
what is called a strain,
enough to rupture the material.

00:28:39.910 --> 00:28:44.790
Now you notice that
if you think about it,

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:47.740
the spring constant is
related to Young's modulus,

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:50.950
because they both describe the
springiness of the material.

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:52.900
But we've got a very
funny shaped object here.

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:55.320
This is not shaped like
an ordinary spring.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.820
And so it turns out that the
relationship between the spring

00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:00.700
constant and the
elastic modulus is

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:03.560
given by the combination
of length, width,

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:04.750
and height of the block.

00:29:04.750 --> 00:29:06.750
And you can read more
about that in the article,

00:29:06.750 --> 00:29:08.470
if you're interested.

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:11.980
On the other hand, in
terms of the stress

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:14.710
for an object of this
shape, this stress

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:19.480
multiplied by the correct
geometrical factors

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:22.910
then gives you the force that is
necessary to break the object.

00:29:22.910 --> 00:29:24.910
So there is a certain
amount of force necessary,

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:28.555
which is proportional
to the rupture modulus.

00:29:28.555 --> 00:29:30.180
And the spring constant
is proportional

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:32.330
to the elastic modulus.

00:29:32.330 --> 00:29:35.320
Now we can say that
that critical deflection

00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:39.790
is just equal to the
spring constant times d0.

00:29:39.790 --> 00:29:40.620
That's the force.

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:42.350
But we already know the force.

00:29:42.350 --> 00:29:45.940
And so immediately, we
can calculate-- we know k,

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:48.560
we know F. So we calculate d0.

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:50.730
And it turns out to be
just the ratio of sigma

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:56.000
to E, the modulus of rupture
to the elastic modulus.

00:29:56.000 --> 00:29:58.220
And then we can
calculate 1/2 k x

00:29:58.220 --> 00:30:00.690
squared, [INAUDIBLE]
meter squared,

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:02.940
and we get this simple formula.

00:30:02.940 --> 00:30:04.670
So the bottom line
is that the amount

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:07.980
of elastic energy necessary
to break the object

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:12.690
is sigma squared over 12 E
times the volume of the block.

00:30:12.690 --> 00:30:14.330
But then remember,
that's the amount

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:16.200
of internal energy
needed, but there

00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:18.470
is a coupling factors
which is calculated having

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:20.270
to do with momentum transfer.

00:30:20.270 --> 00:30:23.030
And that means that the
initial kinetic energy we need

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:26.510
is actually equal to this
mass factor times that,

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:28.247
which can be much bigger.

00:30:28.247 --> 00:30:29.830
Much more energy is
needed, especially

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:32.240
in the case of a heavy
object like concrete.

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:34.670
Next slide, please.

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:35.390
Next slide.

00:30:35.390 --> 00:30:37.655
So finally, I made a
little table for you.

00:30:37.655 --> 00:30:39.650
And this table shows
the parameters.

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:42.180
First of all, I put three
things here-- wood, that's

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:44.690
the piece of wood I showed you.

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:46.660
Concrete-- that's
the patio block.

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:48.740
And then I also put
on a piece of bone

00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:51.230
to give you
information about that.

00:30:51.230 --> 00:30:54.860
Now notice that
wood is the units

00:30:54.860 --> 00:30:57.870
of newtons per square
meter times 10 to the 8th.

00:30:57.870 --> 00:30:59.390
Wood has a value of 1.

00:30:59.390 --> 00:31:01.080
Concrete is much different.

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:02.750
It's 20 times stiffer.

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:04.940
And notice, just for
interest, that bone

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:07.350
is five times
different than that.

00:31:07.350 --> 00:31:10.270
So bone is a very
elastically still material.

00:31:10.270 --> 00:31:13.110
Then in terms of the stresses,
the break [INAUDIBLE],,

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:16.230
notice that wood and concrete
aren't all that different.

00:31:16.230 --> 00:31:17.350
But look at bone.

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:17.960
Look at that.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:21.410
That's a factor of 50 different.

00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:24.260
So bone is a very, very
hard material, much harder

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:26.120
than concrete, actually.

00:31:26.120 --> 00:31:28.220
And in addition,
if we have time,

00:31:28.220 --> 00:31:30.650
we'll also talk about the
effects of orientation, which

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:34.250
can further enhance the
advantage in breaking

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:36.770
the object rather
than your fist.

00:31:36.770 --> 00:31:39.380
So now, [INAUDIBLE] calculated
the deflection distance,

00:31:39.380 --> 00:31:44.037
the internal elastic energy,
and these other [INAUDIBLE],,

00:31:44.037 --> 00:31:45.120
these are in your handout.

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:47.180
I won't probably go
over them, but just

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:50.980
to say that to break
a piece of wood

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:54.900
takes the velocity of the order
of 4 meters per second, which

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:58.220
a beginner could probably do.

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:00.470
And it takes an energy
of around 6 joules.

00:32:00.470 --> 00:32:02.470
These numbers shouldn't
be taken too accurately,

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:04.928
because there's much variations
in the materials from piece

00:32:04.928 --> 00:32:05.990
to piece.

00:32:05.990 --> 00:32:08.630
On the other hand, it turns
out that this analysis

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:10.400
is a little bit simplified.

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:14.150
To break a piece of concrete
takes 8 or 9 meters per second.

00:32:14.150 --> 00:32:16.050
Only an expert could do it.

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:21.230
And the amount of energy
is about 9 or 10 joules.

00:32:21.230 --> 00:32:22.340
So much for the theory.

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:25.910
Let's get into some
experiment here.

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:30.980
And I think that
I will begin here

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:35.090
by demonstrating a hammer fist
strike of a pile of boards

00:32:35.090 --> 00:32:36.038
over here.

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:48.117
As I talk, breaking is not
an essential part of karate.

00:32:48.117 --> 00:32:50.700
And I want to explain that the
only reason that we demonstrate

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:53.195
these techniques is because
it's interesting physics.

00:32:53.195 --> 00:32:55.440
And it really shows
the amount of force

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:57.270
that you can put
in the techniques.

00:32:57.270 --> 00:32:59.940
I'd also like to ask you during
these breaking demonstrations

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:01.934
for extreme quiet.

00:33:01.934 --> 00:33:02.922
Thank you.

00:33:08.356 --> 00:33:09.838
This is a hammer fist.

00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:22.188
Hyah!

00:33:22.188 --> 00:33:25.152
[APPLAUSE]

