00:35:53.050 --> 00:35:54.710
Sparring is a part
of karate which

00:35:54.710 --> 00:35:58.240
is used between experts
to be able to practice

00:35:58.240 --> 00:36:02.250
your techniques and be
sure that they're all good,

00:36:02.250 --> 00:36:04.162
and so forth, and so on.

00:36:04.162 --> 00:36:05.870
And so I'd like to
ask David and Jonathan

00:36:05.870 --> 00:36:11.190
to come out and demonstrate a
little bit of sparring, just to

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:11.940
[INAUDIBLE].

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:22.238
Just very briefly, bow.

00:36:22.238 --> 00:36:24.130
Set.

00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:24.630
Hashime.

00:36:24.630 --> 00:36:27.350
[VOCALIZING]

00:36:45.790 --> 00:36:46.400
Mate.

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:47.856
Mate.

00:36:47.856 --> 00:36:50.724
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:55.030 --> 00:37:02.650
So I'd just like to conclude by
explaining to you-- let me just

00:37:02.650 --> 00:37:06.450
show the last slide here.

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:08.820
Can you please give
me the next slide.

00:37:13.360 --> 00:37:16.420
Oh, yes, let me first
show you breaking.

00:37:16.420 --> 00:37:19.900
We actually had a strobe
movie, and I unfortunately

00:37:19.900 --> 00:37:21.280
could not locate it.

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:25.690
But here is a
picture of a karateka

00:37:25.690 --> 00:37:28.300
which is breaking a
piece of patio block

00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:29.580
like the one you saw.

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:33.640
And each of these pictures
are one millisecond apart.

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:35.400
And you can see that
the fist is actually

00:37:35.400 --> 00:37:42.190
in contact with the target
for about five milliseconds.

00:37:42.190 --> 00:37:44.412
And you can see that there
are these four little dots

00:37:44.412 --> 00:37:46.870
have been placed on the fist,
that we can use for measuring

00:37:46.870 --> 00:37:49.450
the kinematics of the strike.

00:37:49.450 --> 00:37:52.240
And you can see here that
even though the fist comes

00:37:52.240 --> 00:37:58.240
in contact here, that it's
four or five milliseconds later

00:37:58.240 --> 00:38:00.545
that the crack starts to occur.

00:38:00.545 --> 00:38:02.170
And you can see that
the crack actually

00:38:02.170 --> 00:38:03.975
occurs from the bottom.

00:38:03.975 --> 00:38:06.100
And the reason why the
crack occurs from the bottom

00:38:06.100 --> 00:38:10.430
is because most materials
are stronger in compression

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:12.460
than in tension.

00:38:12.460 --> 00:38:16.040
And so it's actually this part
that actually breaks, always.

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:20.080
Now it's really a shame that
I couldn't find the movie,

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:23.470
because in the movie, you
see that the fist really

00:38:23.470 --> 00:38:25.820
does not behave like a
piece of bone at all.

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:29.170
It actually behaves more
like a rubber eraser.

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:31.120
It completely crunches up.

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:32.920
And then as the
strike occurs, you

00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:36.370
see this pulse of
muscle wave, which

00:38:36.370 --> 00:38:37.960
is going right up
the arm, something

00:38:37.960 --> 00:38:40.000
that was extremely unexpected.

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:41.450
May I have the
next slide, please.

00:38:45.050 --> 00:38:49.550
Now here, we have
calculated by just measuring

00:38:49.550 --> 00:38:52.840
the motion of that dot.

00:38:52.840 --> 00:38:55.580
The displacement
during the strike--

00:38:55.580 --> 00:38:57.440
this time scale is--

00:38:57.440 --> 00:39:02.070
each square corresponds
to five milliseconds.

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:05.930
And now, by taking the rate
of change of the position,

00:39:05.930 --> 00:39:07.733
we can get the velocity.

00:39:07.733 --> 00:39:09.650
And then by taking the
rate of change of that,

00:39:09.650 --> 00:39:11.872
we can get the
acceleration of the hand.

00:39:11.872 --> 00:39:13.830
And you can see that at
the time of the strike,

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:17.090
the hand undergoes a huge
acceleration of 3,000

00:39:17.090 --> 00:39:18.890
to 4,000 meters per second.

00:39:18.890 --> 00:39:22.310
That's an enormous
acceleration or deceleration.

00:39:22.310 --> 00:39:25.050
And it quickly comes to rest
after that, or essentially

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:26.230
at rest.

00:39:26.230 --> 00:39:27.830
And may I have the
next slide, please.

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:31.220
Next slide, please.

00:39:31.220 --> 00:39:36.040
[INAUDIBLE] And
here's a picture.

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:37.790
I wanted to show you
a picture of actually

00:39:37.790 --> 00:39:39.710
some incomplete breaks.

00:39:39.710 --> 00:39:42.620
And here's what happens
if you don't exactly

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:45.193
hit the boards in the center.

00:39:45.193 --> 00:39:47.360
What happens is that first
of all, you can see that.

00:39:47.360 --> 00:39:50.390
The wave motion actually
goes to the center

00:39:50.390 --> 00:39:53.068
but it's much, much harder if
you don't hit it in the center

00:39:53.068 --> 00:39:54.360
to be able to do that breaking.

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:56.720
You can see the same
thing here with concrete

00:39:56.720 --> 00:39:58.700
as you can with the boards.

00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:01.730
And also, it's
interesting that here, you

00:40:01.730 --> 00:40:05.510
can see that the hand has only
moved down two or three boards.

00:40:05.510 --> 00:40:08.120
And you can see that the boards
have been broken all the way

00:40:08.120 --> 00:40:10.250
down to the next to last one.

00:40:10.250 --> 00:40:12.950
And that shows that
actually, in a complicated

00:40:12.950 --> 00:40:17.030
situation like this, the
first board actually breaks

00:40:17.030 --> 00:40:18.990
the second and the second
one breaks the third,

00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:20.108
and so forth, and so on.

00:40:20.108 --> 00:40:20.650
Next, please.

00:40:24.770 --> 00:40:26.630
Finally, let me just mention--

00:40:26.630 --> 00:40:29.390
in your article, you can
read more about this--

00:40:29.390 --> 00:40:32.390
that positioning is very,
very important in karate,

00:40:32.390 --> 00:40:34.220
because the geometrical
factors that I

00:40:34.220 --> 00:40:38.210
talked about before,
which can be optimized

00:40:38.210 --> 00:40:45.170
to be able to protect
the bone and to have

00:40:45.170 --> 00:40:47.900
a maximum effect on the target.

00:40:47.900 --> 00:40:50.330
And if you look at your
handout, at the very end

00:40:50.330 --> 00:40:54.350
of your handout, you'll
find that in fact,

00:40:54.350 --> 00:40:58.480
if you orient your foot
correctly in a knife foot

00:40:58.480 --> 00:41:06.080
technique like this, then it
takes 2,000 times as much force

00:41:06.080 --> 00:41:10.400
to be able to break
this object, the bone,

00:41:10.400 --> 00:41:15.890
as it does to be able to
break the concrete block.

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:20.080
So bone is quite a
remarkable object.

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:21.920
So in conclusion,
let me just say

00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:26.630
that I hope that you've learned
a little bit about physics

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:27.470
here.

00:41:27.470 --> 00:41:30.848
I also-- just done [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:30.848 --> 00:41:33.140
I also hope that you learned
a little bit about karate,

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:38.270
and I hope that you will go away
realizing that karate is not

00:41:38.270 --> 00:41:41.380
just something which is
used to beat people up,

00:41:41.380 --> 00:41:46.580
but it really has many important
aesthetic applications,

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:47.720
as well.

00:41:47.720 --> 00:41:50.930
Finally, I'd like to thank my
colleagues for participating

00:41:50.930 --> 00:41:52.454
in this demonstration.

00:41:52.454 --> 00:41:55.436
[APPLAUSE]

