00:44:26.790 --> 00:44:33.120
which is going to discuss the
applications of Newton's laws

00:44:33.120 --> 00:44:35.940
to karate.

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:39.990
And one of the questions that
we're going to talk about

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:42.120
is how--

00:44:42.120 --> 00:44:43.740
could people please settle down.

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:48.820
Please just-- one
of the questions

00:44:48.820 --> 00:44:52.950
we're going to talk about
is how can a piece of bone

00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:57.720
break a piece of concrete rather
than the reverse happening?

00:44:57.720 --> 00:45:00.318
I mean, of course, occasionally
the reverse does happen,

00:45:00.318 --> 00:45:01.860
but hopefully, it
won't happen today.

00:45:09.020 --> 00:45:12.135
So we want to discuss not only
the kinematics of breaking,

00:45:12.135 --> 00:45:13.760
the dynamics of
breaking, but we wanted

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:16.680
to tell you a little bit about
karate and some other things.

00:45:16.680 --> 00:45:20.990
So in order to do that,
I've asked some colleagues

00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:21.830
to join me.

00:45:21.830 --> 00:45:22.970
Would you please come out?

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:35.870
I'd like to introduce
David Feld, Alexandra Feld,

00:45:35.870 --> 00:45:37.640
and Jonathan Feld.

00:45:37.640 --> 00:45:48.060
[INAUDIBLE] We are all
students of the martial arts.

00:45:48.060 --> 00:45:54.600
And I would like to begin by
saying that our teacher was

00:45:54.600 --> 00:45:56.970
a man named Ron McNair.

00:45:56.970 --> 00:45:58.740
And may I have the
first slide, please.

00:46:02.810 --> 00:46:06.400
And this lecture is dedicated
to the memory of Ron McNair.

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:08.530
Ron McNair was a
graduate student

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:12.620
in the Physics Department he
went on to become an astronaut.

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:14.380
He was our karate teacher.

00:46:14.380 --> 00:46:18.700
And unfortunately he died
in the Challenger crash

00:46:18.700 --> 00:46:20.330
several years ago.

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:25.920
And he was really a
very special person.

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:26.790
Next slide, please.

00:46:37.430 --> 00:46:44.060
Karate is a Japanese word
which means "empty hand."

00:46:44.060 --> 00:46:48.620
It's a form of weaponless
self-defense, which

00:46:48.620 --> 00:46:54.460
was actually preceded
by many other forms

00:46:54.460 --> 00:47:01.150
of weaponless fighting, which
occurred in Asia and the Orient

00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:04.190
for more than 2,000 years.

00:47:04.190 --> 00:47:08.120
And the origins of karate
were a mixture of religious,

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:11.750
philosophical, and military.

00:47:11.750 --> 00:47:14.000
And interestingly,
karate was not

00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:18.235
even limited to the
eastern part of the world.

00:47:18.235 --> 00:47:19.610
Here's a picture
from the Vatican

00:47:19.610 --> 00:47:28.010
Museum of depicting a story,
a legend which occurs 400 BC.

00:47:28.010 --> 00:47:30.530
It's about the two
famous Greek fighters,

00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:33.170
Damoxenos and Kruegas.

00:47:33.170 --> 00:47:36.680
And here we see Kruegas
fighting Damoxenos

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:39.620
And what happened was
they fought all day long.

00:47:39.620 --> 00:47:41.210
And neither one
could beat the other

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:42.560
of these two great wrestlers.

00:47:42.560 --> 00:47:43.977
So finally, at the
end of the day,

00:47:43.977 --> 00:47:47.680
they decided they would
decide it by a single blow.

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:56.200
And so first, Damoxenos
said that he would like

00:47:56.200 --> 00:47:58.910
to strike Kruegas in the head.

00:47:58.910 --> 00:48:03.760
And he struck a powerful blow,
but Kruegas withstood it.

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:06.550
And so then Kruegas
asked Damoxenos

00:48:06.550 --> 00:48:09.293
to raise his hand,
which he raised his arm.

00:48:09.293 --> 00:48:10.960
And here's the thing
I want to show you,

00:48:10.960 --> 00:48:14.980
this knife hand strike,
which he then delivered.

00:48:14.980 --> 00:48:20.050
And I won't go into
the medical details,

00:48:20.050 --> 00:48:24.550
but suffice to say that
Kruegas won the battle,

00:48:24.550 --> 00:48:31.930
and became the
famous Greek warrior.

00:48:31.930 --> 00:48:36.460
So this shows that those
ancient techniques were actually

00:48:36.460 --> 00:48:38.500
practiced in the West
as well as the East.

00:48:41.290 --> 00:48:43.070
They occur in all parts of Asia.

00:48:43.070 --> 00:48:47.270
They occur in Burma,
China, and India.

00:48:47.270 --> 00:48:49.810
But what happened was that
modern karate was developed,

00:48:49.810 --> 00:48:53.020
actually, in Okinawa in
the 17th century, when

00:48:53.020 --> 00:48:56.530
the Japanese invaded the
island, and prevented

00:48:56.530 --> 00:48:59.650
the feudal practices
which were then going on.

00:48:59.650 --> 00:49:01.600
So the Okinawans,
in order to continue

00:49:01.600 --> 00:49:05.110
doing their sport
and their fighting,

00:49:05.110 --> 00:49:08.080
they developed weaponless
forms of fighting.

00:49:08.080 --> 00:49:12.640
And then in early 1900s,
karate was taken from Okinawa

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:18.060
to Japan, where it developed
into a very formalized subject.

00:49:18.060 --> 00:49:20.890
And today, we want to
tell you a little bit

00:49:20.890 --> 00:49:25.598
about the study of karate.

00:49:25.598 --> 00:49:27.640
We want to tell you a
little about the philosophy

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:28.870
behind karate.

00:49:28.870 --> 00:49:31.780
We want to discuss the physical
aspects of karate, of course.

00:49:31.780 --> 00:49:33.680
That's the most important thing.

00:49:33.680 --> 00:49:36.340
But I don't want you
to go away, certainly,

00:49:36.340 --> 00:49:41.270
thinking that destroying
objects is the important thing

00:49:41.270 --> 00:49:43.400
in karate, because it's not.

00:49:43.400 --> 00:49:46.760
Destroying objects is only a
way of gauging the effectiveness

00:49:46.760 --> 00:49:48.230
of the techniques.

00:49:48.230 --> 00:49:51.260
And also, I don't want you
to get the idea that karate

00:49:51.260 --> 00:49:54.050
is a sport or an
activity which is

00:49:54.050 --> 00:49:57.510
done to be able
to be a tough guy

00:49:57.510 --> 00:49:59.810
or to go out and beat people up.

00:49:59.810 --> 00:50:03.800
Karate is a very defensive art.

00:50:03.800 --> 00:50:05.360
It's inherently defensive.

00:50:05.360 --> 00:50:08.437
And I will explain
that more as we go on.

00:50:08.437 --> 00:50:10.520
So in this lecture, what
we're going to talk about

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:13.210
is, first of all, some
of the basic techniques.

00:50:13.210 --> 00:50:17.180
And then we're going to
talk about the kata, which

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:20.410
are perhaps the most beautiful
and interesting part of karate,

00:50:20.410 --> 00:50:21.590
to me, at least.

00:50:21.590 --> 00:50:25.640
Then we're going to discuss the
theory and experimental aspects

00:50:25.640 --> 00:50:27.840
of breaking of objects.

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:30.990
And then we'll discuss a
few other things, as well.

00:50:30.990 --> 00:50:33.920
So I would like to
now begin by asking

00:50:33.920 --> 00:50:36.110
David and Jonathan to
come out and demonstrate

00:50:36.110 --> 00:50:37.702
some basic techniques.

00:50:48.870 --> 00:50:51.000
So let us now begin
by demonstrating

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:53.460
the most basic
technique in all karate,

00:50:53.460 --> 00:50:55.530
the front forward
punch, Seiken Zuki.

00:50:58.650 --> 00:51:03.220
Now this is a good example of
a typical karate technique.

00:51:03.220 --> 00:51:05.560
It's very, very different
from the Western sports

00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:06.577
which are practiced.

00:51:06.577 --> 00:51:08.410
In Western sports, the
first thing you learn

00:51:08.410 --> 00:51:11.620
is about follow-through in
baseball, or golf, or boxing.

00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:13.880
But in karate, there is no
follow through whatsoever

00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:15.010
on almost every technique.

00:51:15.010 --> 00:51:17.110
Instead, there is focus.

00:51:17.110 --> 00:51:20.800
This technique is delivered
with a high velocity.

00:51:20.800 --> 00:51:23.470
It terminates in a
very short period,

00:51:23.470 --> 00:51:25.090
right inside the target.

00:51:25.090 --> 00:51:27.250
And the focus is very
important in imparting

00:51:27.250 --> 00:51:32.170
the maximum amount of momentum
and force to the target.

00:51:32.170 --> 00:51:35.920
And the ancient orientals
who studied this

00:51:35.920 --> 00:51:39.460
had no notion of Newton's
laws, but they still

00:51:39.460 --> 00:51:42.580
developed techniques
which, when we study them

00:51:42.580 --> 00:51:46.750
by modern methods, we can see
that these techniques really

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:49.220
make nearly optimal
use of the human body.

00:51:49.220 --> 00:51:53.500
So let's demonstrate
that in a faster method.

00:51:53.500 --> 00:51:54.460
FELD: [YELLS]

00:51:54.460 --> 00:51:57.040
FELD: Okay, now
karate techniques

00:51:57.040 --> 00:51:59.467
are also very highly
prescribed, very formalized.

00:51:59.467 --> 00:52:01.300
For example, there are
many, many techniques

00:52:01.300 --> 00:52:05.800
in many parts of the
hands, feet, head, elbows.

00:52:05.800 --> 00:52:10.390
Many parts of the body can be
used to deliver techniques.

00:52:10.390 --> 00:52:12.460
Even with the hand, there
are many, many parts

00:52:12.460 --> 00:52:15.250
of the hand which can be used
to deliver karate techniques,

00:52:15.250 --> 00:52:17.770
but each one is done in
a very prescribed way.

00:52:17.770 --> 00:52:20.410
So for example, you can also
do a front forward punch

00:52:20.410 --> 00:52:23.728
to the face rather than
to the solar plexus.

00:52:23.728 --> 00:52:24.770
Demonstrate that, please.

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:32.303
FELD: [YELLS]

00:52:32.303 --> 00:52:33.970
FELD: Just as we have
strikes in karate,

00:52:33.970 --> 00:52:37.990
we also have ways of defending
ourselves against strikes.

00:52:37.990 --> 00:52:42.820
And now, we'll have
a series of Jonathan

00:52:42.820 --> 00:52:45.340
will demonstrate
some blocks, which

00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:46.810
are used to defend the strikes.

00:52:46.810 --> 00:52:48.020
Go ahead.

00:52:48.020 --> 00:52:50.890
FELD: [VOCALIZING]

00:52:50.890 --> 00:52:52.330
FELD: So this is
a mid-body block.

00:52:52.330 --> 00:52:54.288
And you can see that
here, the principle is not

00:52:54.288 --> 00:52:58.000
to meet it with force on
force, but instead to provide

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:00.820
a certain amount of transverse
force and transverse

00:53:00.820 --> 00:53:05.500
momentum, which will deflect
the oncoming technique,

00:53:05.500 --> 00:53:09.970
and render it ineffective.

00:53:09.970 --> 00:53:11.830
There are many, many
other techniques,

00:53:11.830 --> 00:53:14.920
and Jonathan will now
demonstrate a hammer fist

00:53:14.920 --> 00:53:16.490
strike, which is another type.

00:53:16.490 --> 00:53:20.494
And David will show how
you defend against that.

00:53:20.494 --> 00:53:30.187
FELD: [VOCALIZING]

00:53:30.187 --> 00:53:32.770
FELD: As I said, other parts of
the body can be used, as well.

00:53:32.770 --> 00:53:34.187
And let us just
demonstrate to you

00:53:34.187 --> 00:53:36.330
now one kick, the
side kick, which

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:38.550
is a very powerful
technique, the Yoko

00:53:38.550 --> 00:53:42.570
Gari to the solar plexus.

00:53:42.570 --> 00:53:45.190
Now let me just show
you here for a moment.

00:53:45.190 --> 00:53:47.970
Notice that here again,
this is a technique

00:53:47.970 --> 00:53:52.380
in which the foot is knifed in
a position in which the very

00:53:52.380 --> 00:53:55.470
narrowest surface area is
presented to the target.

00:53:55.470 --> 00:53:58.650
The toes are curled so that
they're out of the way,

00:53:58.650 --> 00:54:01.860
and not in danger of being
broken off in the technique.

00:54:01.860 --> 00:54:04.647
And this can deliver a great
deal of energy and momentum,

00:54:04.647 --> 00:54:05.980
as we'll talk about in a moment.

00:54:05.980 --> 00:54:08.698
Now do it at full speed.

00:54:08.698 --> 00:54:11.900
FELD: [YELLS]

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:14.640
FELD: Thank you very much.

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:22.310
Now I'd like to have
the next slide, please.

00:54:28.950 --> 00:54:31.400
And now, I want to show
you some measurements

00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:35.420
that we've taken of
various karate techniques

00:54:35.420 --> 00:54:38.060
so that we could get some
feeling of how much energy is

00:54:38.060 --> 00:54:40.060
available in a karate strike.

00:54:40.060 --> 00:54:42.140
And here's a picture of--

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:47.060
this actually is a set of strobe
photos taken in Doc Edgerton's

00:54:47.060 --> 00:54:53.450
lab, of a Seiken Zuki, a
front forward punch, taken

00:54:53.450 --> 00:54:55.400
at exposures with
intervals of one

00:54:55.400 --> 00:54:57.570
millisecond from one another.

00:54:57.570 --> 00:55:00.770
And then, by just taking
the location of the fist,

00:55:00.770 --> 00:55:02.840
and plotting this as
a function of time

00:55:02.840 --> 00:55:05.180
or plotting it as a
function of distance,

00:55:05.180 --> 00:55:06.860
we can measure
the velocity which

00:55:06.860 --> 00:55:08.633
is developed in this technique.

00:55:08.633 --> 00:55:10.050
And here you can
see, for example,

00:55:10.050 --> 00:55:12.890
this particular
technique had a velocity

00:55:12.890 --> 00:55:18.120
of about 7 meters per
second at a distance which

00:55:18.120 --> 00:55:21.770
was about 85% percent
of the distance

00:55:21.770 --> 00:55:25.940
through the technique, and
maybe one or two centimeters

00:55:25.940 --> 00:55:28.100
inside the target,
which is just about

00:55:28.100 --> 00:55:30.890
right for karate techniques.

00:55:30.890 --> 00:55:32.900
So here, you have
a typical number,

00:55:32.900 --> 00:55:37.220
like maybe 10 meters per second
as the amount of velocity

00:55:37.220 --> 00:55:39.830
you can develop in
a karate technique.

00:55:39.830 --> 00:55:42.470
And if you consider a hand,
for example, the mass of a hand

00:55:42.470 --> 00:55:45.290
is a little bit less
than a kilogram,

00:55:45.290 --> 00:55:48.230
typically, maybe
7/10 of a kilogram.

00:55:48.230 --> 00:55:50.845
And so if we figure out the
amount of kinetic energy

00:55:50.845 --> 00:55:52.220
which is available
there, we know

00:55:52.220 --> 00:55:55.410
it's 1/2 mass times
the velocity squared.

00:55:55.410 --> 00:55:58.740
Let's take the velocity
as 10 meters per second.

00:55:58.740 --> 00:56:03.920
So 10 squared is 100 times
1 is 100 divided by 2 is 50.

00:56:03.920 --> 00:56:06.350
So typically, there's
about 50 joules of energy

00:56:06.350 --> 00:56:10.920
available in a karate technique
when delivered by an expert.

00:56:10.920 --> 00:56:14.260
Can I have the
next slide, please.

00:56:14.260 --> 00:56:16.830
Now here, we have a
hammer fist strike,

00:56:16.830 --> 00:56:19.380
in this case breaking some wood.

00:56:19.380 --> 00:56:22.080
And here again, we've
studied the motion here.

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:26.050
And over a period of
100 or 150 milliseconds,

00:56:26.050 --> 00:56:27.120
which is pretty slow--

00:56:27.120 --> 00:56:30.720
this is a slow technique,
but a powerful one--

00:56:30.720 --> 00:56:34.290
something like velocity
of 12 meters per second

00:56:34.290 --> 00:56:35.490
was developed.

00:56:35.490 --> 00:56:38.360
Next, please.

00:56:38.360 --> 00:56:41.010
And here is a list of
various techniques--

00:56:41.010 --> 00:56:45.230
the Seiken Zuki, the hammer fist
strike, a knife hand strike,

00:56:45.230 --> 00:56:46.490
various kicks.

00:56:46.490 --> 00:56:49.010
And you can see in each case
that the peak speeds, which

00:56:49.010 --> 00:56:52.100
are reached right inside
the target in each case

00:56:52.100 --> 00:56:54.320
when delivered by an
expert, are of the order

00:56:54.320 --> 00:56:56.755
of 10 meters per second.

00:56:56.755 --> 00:56:58.130
And there's some
more information

00:56:58.130 --> 00:57:00.440
about that in your
handout in case

00:57:00.440 --> 00:57:02.980
you're interested in
more facts about that.

00:57:02.980 --> 00:57:05.610
May I have the
next slide, please?

00:57:05.610 --> 00:57:09.150
Fine, enough for the
slides right now.

00:57:09.150 --> 00:57:12.960
So now, I would like
to go on to what

00:57:12.960 --> 00:57:16.740
I think is the most interesting
part of karate, the kata.

00:57:16.740 --> 00:57:24.140
And the kata are very
similar to special types

00:57:24.140 --> 00:57:27.320
of ballet sequences
of classical ballet.

00:57:27.320 --> 00:57:32.000
In the kata, the karateka
goes through a series

00:57:32.000 --> 00:57:39.020
of motions in which he or
she is protecting herself

00:57:39.020 --> 00:57:42.960
against a series of
imaginary attackers.

00:57:42.960 --> 00:57:47.065
And the moves are highly
prescribed, highly formalized.

00:57:47.065 --> 00:57:48.690
Kata are something
in which you're only

00:57:48.690 --> 00:57:49.890
competing against yourself.

00:57:49.890 --> 00:57:53.290
You strive for perfection,
just as you would in ballet.

00:57:53.290 --> 00:57:56.040
It's really a way of trying
to do beautiful things

00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:57.210
with your body.

00:57:57.210 --> 00:58:00.440
And some of the kata
are quite beautiful.

00:58:00.440 --> 00:58:02.580
There are about
50 of these kata.

00:58:02.580 --> 00:58:05.730
And interestingly,
every one of them

00:58:05.730 --> 00:58:08.880
begins with at least
one defensive technique.

00:58:08.880 --> 00:58:13.260
And that's typical of karate.

00:58:13.260 --> 00:58:15.300
Karate is really
a defensive art.

00:58:15.300 --> 00:58:18.210
It is not an aggressive
kind of an art.

00:58:21.810 --> 00:58:24.960
So let me now ask
Alexandra if she

00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:28.950
will demonstrate the beginner
kata, Taikyoku number one.

00:58:28.950 --> 00:58:30.450
Alexandra, would
you please do that.

00:58:35.928 --> 00:58:37.422
FELD: Taikyoku number one.

00:58:49.374 --> 00:58:52.362
[VOCALIZES]

00:59:10.788 --> 00:59:13.776
[APPLAUSE]

00:59:16.830 --> 00:59:18.770
FELD: Alexandra, could
you please tell us

00:59:18.770 --> 00:59:22.170
about the very last thing
that you did in the kata.

00:59:22.170 --> 00:59:25.880
FELD: I am a practitioner
of the martial arts.

00:59:25.880 --> 00:59:27.960
I bear no weapons.

00:59:27.960 --> 00:59:32.030
I pray I will not have to use my
karate on anybody, but if I do,

00:59:32.030 --> 00:59:33.170
I am ready.

00:59:33.170 --> 00:59:34.968
FELD: Thank you.

00:59:34.968 --> 00:59:37.884
[APPLAUSE]

00:59:41.300 --> 00:59:44.337
As you can see, there are many
symbolic gestures in karate.

00:59:44.337 --> 00:59:45.920
And I also want to
mention that karate

00:59:45.920 --> 00:59:49.400
is one of those sports
where age, sex, weight,

00:59:49.400 --> 00:59:51.374
height are really no barrier.

00:59:51.374 --> 00:59:55.280
And in karate tournaments, the
littlest people and the biggest

00:59:55.280 --> 00:59:57.500
people can all compete
together depending

00:59:57.500 --> 01:00:00.350
upon their belt color,
rather than upon their age.

01:00:00.350 --> 01:00:03.140
Now I'd like to get into
some more advanced kata.

01:00:03.140 --> 01:00:05.480
And I'd like to now
show you a kata.

01:00:05.480 --> 01:00:06.980
Jonathan will show
us a card which

01:00:06.980 --> 01:00:11.240
is called Gankaku, which
means "crane on a rock."

01:00:11.240 --> 01:00:18.440
The ancient Asian karate
masters studied nature

01:00:18.440 --> 01:00:22.550
and studied animals to
understand much about how

01:00:22.550 --> 01:00:24.420
to optimize techniques.

01:00:24.420 --> 01:00:26.810
And this particular
kata was actually

01:00:26.810 --> 01:00:28.925
based on the study of the crane.

01:00:28.925 --> 01:00:30.800
And I'd like you to look
very, very carefully

01:00:30.800 --> 01:00:32.390
at the end of this kata.

01:00:32.390 --> 01:00:34.820
See if you can see
the four stances

01:00:34.820 --> 01:00:36.600
of the one-legged crane.

01:00:36.600 --> 01:00:37.100
Jonathan.

01:00:44.240 --> 01:00:46.190
FELD: Gankaku, crane on a rock.

01:00:49.118 --> 01:00:52.046
[VOCALIZING]

01:01:33.180 --> 01:01:36.120
[APPLAUSE]

01:01:38.570 --> 01:01:39.800
FELD: Thanks, John.

01:01:39.800 --> 01:01:42.770
Now we'd like to now
demonstrate another technique.

01:01:42.770 --> 01:01:47.480
David is going to demonstrate a
kata called Bassai, Bassai Dai.

01:01:47.480 --> 01:01:51.500
And Bassai is a kata
which really demonstrates,

01:01:51.500 --> 01:01:54.740
once again, the defensive
nature of karate.

01:01:54.740 --> 01:01:57.740
This is a very powerful
kata, and yet you

01:01:57.740 --> 01:02:01.400
will notice that of the
42 moves of the kata,

01:02:01.400 --> 01:02:05.000
the first eight of them are
purely defensive techniques,

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:05.890
are purely blocks.

01:02:05.890 --> 01:02:09.800
So before any kind of
counter-attacks occur,

01:02:09.800 --> 01:02:14.450
the karateka makes an attempt
to avoid any kind of conflict

01:02:14.450 --> 01:02:16.010
by making eight blocks.

01:02:16.010 --> 01:02:18.170
And only after
blocking his opponents,

01:02:18.170 --> 01:02:20.450
and finding that he
has no way out does

01:02:20.450 --> 01:02:23.580
he find that he has
to go on the attack.

01:02:23.580 --> 01:02:24.080
David.

01:02:33.062 --> 01:02:36.056
FELD: [VOCALIZING]

01:03:22.962 --> 01:03:25.956
[APPLAUSE]

01:03:41.425 --> 01:03:44.419
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:04:14.858 --> 01:04:17.353
[CHEERS]

01:04:22.842 --> 01:04:27.270
[INAUDIBLE] These are
difficult to get on.

01:04:27.270 --> 01:04:31.080
I'd like to now demonstrate
a kata called Tekki.

01:04:31.080 --> 01:04:35.100
And "tekki" means "to
penetrate a fortress."

01:04:35.100 --> 01:04:36.750
It's a linear kata.

01:04:36.750 --> 01:04:39.030
It's a very strong
kata, and it's again

01:04:39.030 --> 01:04:40.220
a very defensive kata.

01:04:58.520 --> 01:04:59.550
Tekki Shodan.

01:05:10.650 --> 01:05:13.620
[VOCALIZES]

01:05:39.193 --> 01:05:42.151
[APPLAUSE]

01:05:48.100 --> 01:05:50.090
I'd like to now
talk a little bit

01:05:50.090 --> 01:05:52.470
about the physics of breaking.

01:05:52.470 --> 01:05:54.440
And in all the excitement,
I forgot my notes.

01:05:54.440 --> 01:05:55.622
Let me go and get them.

01:06:12.350 --> 01:06:22.670
So let's discuss how
it is concrete patio

01:06:22.670 --> 01:06:26.300
blocks like this certainly
seem like massive objects.

01:06:26.300 --> 01:06:28.250
And it's hard to believe
that a person can

01:06:28.250 --> 01:06:30.500
break one or many of them.

01:06:30.500 --> 01:06:32.580
And yet it's possible,
and we should understand

01:06:32.580 --> 01:06:36.430
from simple point of
view of Newton's laws

01:06:36.430 --> 01:06:38.430
could be brought about.

01:06:38.430 --> 01:06:39.962
May I have the
first slide, please?

01:06:39.962 --> 01:06:41.170
It's just [INAUDIBLE] lights.

01:06:43.960 --> 01:06:46.530
Leave that slide.

01:06:46.530 --> 01:06:49.520
So basically, the
problem of breaking

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:57.840
is a problem of wave motion,
exciting waves in a block.

01:06:57.840 --> 01:07:02.300
And a block is really considered
to be like a violin strings.

01:07:02.300 --> 01:07:04.350
And if you're interested
in some of the details,

01:07:04.350 --> 01:07:07.680
you can read the handout, which
discusses this in some detail.

01:07:07.680 --> 01:07:11.270
But I'd like to present today
a sort of simplified picture

01:07:11.270 --> 01:07:15.600
in which we consider it instead
a problem of momentum transfer.

01:07:15.600 --> 01:07:17.930
And so basically,
here's the idea.

01:07:17.930 --> 01:07:21.750
We have a fist that's impacting
on a piece of concrete

01:07:21.750 --> 01:07:23.440
or so forth and so on.

01:07:23.440 --> 01:07:26.303
And we want to understand
exactly how objects break,

01:07:26.303 --> 01:07:27.470
what it takes to break them.

01:07:27.470 --> 01:07:31.070
May I have next slide, please.

01:07:31.070 --> 01:07:34.280
So here we have
our board or block

01:07:34.280 --> 01:07:37.370
with dimensions l, h and w.

01:07:37.370 --> 01:07:39.800
And the block
undergoes a deflection

01:07:39.800 --> 01:07:45.395
when struck with the fist or
with a foot, as shown here.

01:07:45.395 --> 01:07:47.500
And basically, as we'll
explain in a moment, when

01:07:47.500 --> 01:07:49.740
that inflection reaches
a critical distance,

01:07:49.740 --> 01:07:51.110
the object breaks.

01:07:51.110 --> 01:07:53.563
But I'm getting
ahead of myself here.

01:07:53.563 --> 01:07:54.980
The first thing
is that the object

01:07:54.980 --> 01:07:57.558
is an object which is elastic.

01:07:57.558 --> 01:07:59.600
You can think about it--
even a piece of concrete

01:07:59.600 --> 01:08:02.480
has a certain amount
of elasticity in it.

01:08:02.480 --> 01:08:04.980
That can be seen, for example,
by taking a piece of concrete

01:08:04.980 --> 01:08:07.740
and halving it with a
hammer, and noticing that it

01:08:07.740 --> 01:08:10.340
can support [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:10.340 --> 01:08:12.150
So it does have
some elastic nature.

01:08:12.150 --> 01:08:15.140
So does bone, And so
does every property.

01:08:15.140 --> 01:08:19.279
And so in a sense, you
can think of this problem

01:08:19.279 --> 01:08:24.609
as really like the problem
of a mass on a spring--

01:08:24.609 --> 01:08:26.810
that really, this
concrete block can

01:08:26.810 --> 01:08:29.229
be thought of as
a mass of concrete

01:08:29.229 --> 01:08:32.000
on a spring of constant k.

01:08:32.000 --> 01:08:35.240
And actually, the mass, the
effective mass is only 1/2

01:08:35.240 --> 01:08:37.010
the mass of the object.

01:08:37.010 --> 01:08:39.350
And the reason why is roughly
because only the middle

01:08:39.350 --> 01:08:40.319
of the object moves.

01:08:40.319 --> 01:08:43.100
The ends are simply
supported, as shown here.

01:08:43.100 --> 01:08:45.870
And they don't participate
in the motion at all.

01:08:45.870 --> 01:08:48.710
So it turns out that if
you study the theory,

01:08:48.710 --> 01:08:50.939
that only half of
the mass comes in.

01:08:50.939 --> 01:08:53.930
And we have to figure out
what the spring constant is

01:08:53.930 --> 01:08:58.250
in terms of the elastic nature
of the object and its geometry.

01:08:58.250 --> 01:09:01.310
So basically, we
can sort of model

01:09:01.310 --> 01:09:03.770
this problem as a
problem of having

01:09:03.770 --> 01:09:09.410
a mass, a fist or a foot, a mass
capital M and velocity capital

01:09:09.410 --> 01:09:14.689
V, which is impacting on the
spring and compressing it.

01:09:14.689 --> 01:09:16.790
So here, we have drawn
the picture of that.

01:09:16.790 --> 01:09:20.000
And I'd like to point out
three phases of the process

01:09:20.000 --> 01:09:22.399
according to the
simplified picture.

01:09:22.399 --> 01:09:25.240
In the first phase,
we have the fist

01:09:25.240 --> 01:09:29.210
with an initial kinetic
energy, 1/2 MV squared,

01:09:29.210 --> 01:09:34.460
which is just about to make
an impact on the block.

01:09:34.460 --> 01:09:37.439
In the second phase,
before the spring has

01:09:37.439 --> 01:09:42.830
had a chance to compress
but after the collision has

01:09:42.830 --> 01:09:46.790
occurred, we have a situation
where the two masses are

01:09:46.790 --> 01:09:49.300
essentially stuck
together in contact,

01:09:49.300 --> 01:09:53.330
and the mass system now is
moving with a velocity V,

01:09:53.330 --> 01:09:56.070
giving a final kinetic
energy [INAUDIBLE]

01:09:56.070 --> 01:09:59.590
M plus m times little v squared.

01:09:59.590 --> 01:10:03.530
Now that system can
then convert this energy

01:10:03.530 --> 01:10:06.870
into elastic energy,
1/2 kx squared.

01:10:06.870 --> 01:10:09.680
And when the compression
is sufficient to compress

01:10:09.680 --> 01:10:12.830
the object to a distance
d0, a critical distance

01:10:12.830 --> 01:10:14.600
that we'll talk
about in a moment,

01:10:14.600 --> 01:10:18.780
then that elastic energy
will break the object.

01:10:18.780 --> 01:10:21.850
Now I want to point
out to you [INAUDIBLE]

01:10:21.850 --> 01:10:24.160
studying momentum
in the last lecture.

01:10:24.160 --> 01:10:26.510
And I want to point
out to you that this

01:10:26.510 --> 01:10:29.960
is a process in which kinetic
energy is actually not

01:10:29.960 --> 01:10:30.650
conserved.

01:10:30.650 --> 01:10:32.510
And I'll show you
that very simply.

01:10:32.510 --> 01:10:36.830
The kinetic energy which is
available here cannot all go

01:10:36.830 --> 01:10:38.570
into the object.

01:10:38.570 --> 01:10:41.090
And that really depends,
to a certain amount,

01:10:41.090 --> 01:10:42.705
on the nature of the object.

01:10:42.705 --> 01:10:44.580
It really depends on
the ratio of the masses,

01:10:44.580 --> 01:10:46.360
as I'll show you now.

01:10:46.360 --> 01:10:48.930
But basically, if you have
a piece of wood like this,

01:10:48.930 --> 01:10:51.390
which is a mass of maybe
a quarter of a kilogram--

01:10:51.390 --> 01:10:53.690
this is 1 inch white pine--

01:10:53.690 --> 01:10:56.720
and you strike it
like this, then

01:10:56.720 --> 01:10:59.590
the fist is much more
massive than the wood.

01:10:59.590 --> 01:11:03.422
And that, as you will see, gives
very effective energy transfer.

01:11:03.422 --> 01:11:05.630
But on the other hand, a
heavy thing like this-- this

01:11:05.630 --> 01:11:10.280
is a concrete patio block
made of number three cement,

01:11:10.280 --> 01:11:14.480
and it has a weight of 6.5 kg.

01:11:14.480 --> 01:11:17.360
And so because the hand
is relatively light

01:11:17.360 --> 01:11:20.330
compared to that, the amount
of energy which is transferred

01:11:20.330 --> 01:11:21.370
is very small.

01:11:21.370 --> 01:11:22.700
I want you to see that.

01:11:26.302 --> 01:11:28.010
The way it works is
this-- let's just use

01:11:28.010 --> 01:11:30.088
the conservation of momentum.

01:11:30.088 --> 01:11:32.130
The initial momentum--
you remember that momentum

01:11:32.130 --> 01:11:34.650
is just the product of
mass times velocity.

01:11:34.650 --> 01:11:38.020
So the initial momentum that
we have is just the mass times

01:11:38.020 --> 01:11:40.630
the velocity of this object.

01:11:40.630 --> 01:11:44.910
But then this is still, so it
has no momentum, [INAUDIBLE]..

01:11:44.910 --> 01:11:47.760
But afterwards, when the two
objects are stuck together

01:11:47.760 --> 01:11:50.730
here, then we have
a momentum which

01:11:50.730 --> 01:11:54.060
is the sum of the masses
times the final velocity.

01:11:54.060 --> 01:11:55.560
And of course, it's
very easy, then,

01:11:55.560 --> 01:11:57.980
to find what that
final velocity is.

01:11:57.980 --> 01:12:02.490
And it's just given by d
times capital M over M plus m.

01:12:02.490 --> 01:12:08.100
Now the final kinetic energy is
just 1/2 times the total mass,

01:12:08.100 --> 01:12:10.370
M plus m, times
little v squared.

01:12:10.370 --> 01:12:12.370
If you then substituted
little v squared,

01:12:12.370 --> 01:12:13.870
you find something
very interesting.

01:12:13.870 --> 01:12:16.950
And that is that in terms of
the initial kinetic energy,

01:12:16.950 --> 01:12:22.020
1/2 capital M capital V squared,
the final kinetic energy

01:12:22.020 --> 01:12:23.370
is less.

01:12:23.370 --> 01:12:27.150
The final kinetic energy is less
than the initial kinetic energy

01:12:27.150 --> 01:12:30.420
by the ratio of the
mass of the hand

01:12:30.420 --> 01:12:33.750
to the sum of the masses
of the hand and the board,

01:12:33.750 --> 01:12:36.165
which shows that if
you have a heavy object

01:12:36.165 --> 01:12:38.040
that you're striking
with, this fraction will

01:12:38.040 --> 01:12:40.170
be a very small
fraction, indicating

01:12:40.170 --> 01:12:42.660
that very little of the
available kinetic energy

01:12:42.660 --> 01:12:45.910
can actually be
transferred to the object.

01:12:45.910 --> 01:12:49.500
Now also notice
that we can get--

01:12:49.500 --> 01:12:52.080
we said that the final
kinetic energy immediately

01:12:52.080 --> 01:12:54.630
could be converted
into elastic energy.

01:12:54.630 --> 01:12:58.540
And so therefore, I can solve
for the initial kinetic energy

01:12:58.540 --> 01:13:00.900
setting it equal
to M plus M over m

01:13:00.900 --> 01:13:03.180
times the final kinetic
energy, and then

01:13:03.180 --> 01:13:05.370
saying that the final
kinetic energy then becomes

01:13:05.370 --> 01:13:09.750
the elastic energy, and
get this very basic formula

01:13:09.750 --> 01:13:14.130
that the initial kinetic energy
needed to break this object

01:13:14.130 --> 01:13:16.530
is equal to whatever that
elastic energy turns out

01:13:16.530 --> 01:13:19.350
to be multiplied by a
number bigger than 1,

01:13:19.350 --> 01:13:23.820
which depends upon the momentum
transfer between the fist

01:13:23.820 --> 01:13:25.170
and the object.

01:13:25.170 --> 01:13:26.710
Can I have the
next slide, please.

01:13:26.710 --> 01:13:28.793
So now what we have to do
is we have to figure out

01:13:28.793 --> 01:13:31.530
what is the spring constant
of this effective system,

01:13:31.530 --> 01:13:34.730
and what is the critical
distance for breaking.

01:13:34.730 --> 01:13:36.510
Now here, we have
to talk a little bit

01:13:36.510 --> 01:13:38.030
about mechanical engineering.

01:13:38.030 --> 01:13:42.750
There are two properties which
actually describe the behavior

01:13:42.750 --> 01:13:45.490
of a material like this.

01:13:45.490 --> 01:13:47.185
The first one is
called Young's modulus,

01:13:47.185 --> 01:13:50.860
and it describes the
elasticity of the object.

01:13:50.860 --> 01:13:53.480
And the next one is called
the modulus of rupture.

01:13:53.480 --> 01:13:56.355
And it is a measure
of how much force

01:13:56.355 --> 01:14:01.470
or how much stress is
necessary to break the object.

01:14:01.470 --> 01:14:03.480
And these are
numbers which really

01:14:03.480 --> 01:14:05.400
are independent of the
shape of the object.

01:14:05.400 --> 01:14:07.260
They just depend
upon the material.

01:14:07.260 --> 01:14:12.270
So Young's modulus, E, has units
of newtons per square meter.

01:14:12.270 --> 01:14:17.030
And the rupture modulus, sigma
0, also has the same units.

01:14:17.030 --> 01:14:20.190
And this one, then, has to do
with what kind of springiness

01:14:20.190 --> 01:14:21.030
the object has.

01:14:21.030 --> 01:14:23.160
And this one is
how much resilience

01:14:23.160 --> 01:14:27.270
it has to-- how much forces
are set up before it breaks.

01:14:27.270 --> 01:14:31.110
Now, you have to put in
the geometry of the object

01:14:31.110 --> 01:14:34.870
to be able to get the
quantities that you really need.

01:14:34.870 --> 01:14:37.790
So if you take this
Young's modulus,

01:14:37.790 --> 01:14:40.890
the elasticity parameter,
and you multiply it

01:14:40.890 --> 01:14:43.410
by the appropriate
shape factor, which

01:14:43.410 --> 01:14:45.250
is a combination of
the length, width,

01:14:45.250 --> 01:14:47.670
and height of the
object, then you find out

01:14:47.670 --> 01:14:50.550
the spring constant
is just this Young's

01:14:50.550 --> 01:14:54.240
modulus times that combination.

01:14:54.240 --> 01:14:57.030
Similarly, for this
particular kind of a geometry,

01:14:57.030 --> 01:14:59.490
when broken at
the top like this,

01:14:59.490 --> 01:15:01.990
the amount of force
necessary to break the object

01:15:01.990 --> 01:15:05.160
is just given by the modulus
of rupture multiplied

01:15:05.160 --> 01:15:08.040
by these same parameters.

01:15:08.040 --> 01:15:11.440
Now, once we have
this formula for F

01:15:11.440 --> 01:15:14.040
and we have this
formula for k, we

01:15:14.040 --> 01:15:17.600
can easily find d0, the
critical deflection,

01:15:17.600 --> 01:15:20.520
by simply saying
F0, which we know,

01:15:20.520 --> 01:15:23.200
and k that we know times d0.

01:15:23.200 --> 01:15:25.910
And that immediately
gives us that d0

01:15:25.910 --> 01:15:28.950
is equal to some
combination of dimensions

01:15:28.950 --> 01:15:35.580
times the modulus of rupture
divided by the elastic modulus.

01:15:35.580 --> 01:15:38.430
And then we can say that
the elastic energy required

01:15:38.430 --> 01:15:42.335
to break the block is
just 1/2 k d0 squared.

01:15:42.335 --> 01:15:44.980
And that turns out to be
this very simple formula--

01:15:44.980 --> 01:15:51.250
sigma 0 squared over 12E times
the volume of the object.

01:15:51.250 --> 01:15:52.840
Now, remember that
we said before

01:15:52.840 --> 01:15:56.862
that this is the internal energy
needed to break the object.

01:15:56.862 --> 01:15:59.070
But on the other hand, in
terms of the initial energy

01:15:59.070 --> 01:16:02.050
available in the fist,
we have to multiply

01:16:02.050 --> 01:16:07.320
this factor of M plus m over
M. So that's the basic idea

01:16:07.320 --> 01:16:08.400
of this simple picture.

01:16:08.400 --> 01:16:10.470
May I have the
next slide, please.

01:16:10.470 --> 01:16:13.820
I just made a little table
here to summarize for you

01:16:13.820 --> 01:16:16.340
what these parameters
are for three things--

01:16:16.340 --> 01:16:18.880
first of all, the wood
I just showed you,

01:16:18.880 --> 01:16:22.100
the concrete that I showed
you and then some bone.

01:16:22.100 --> 01:16:25.880
So first of all, the springiness
parameter, the Young's modulus,

01:16:25.880 --> 01:16:29.250
this is in units of 10 to the
8th newtons per square meter.

01:16:29.250 --> 01:16:31.900
For wood, it's
about 1, but you can

01:16:31.900 --> 01:16:37.040
see that it's 50 times
bigger for concrete,

01:16:37.040 --> 01:16:41.187
maybe not 50 times bigger,
but 20 times bigger.

01:16:41.187 --> 01:16:43.270
And so you can see that
concrete is a much stiffer

01:16:43.270 --> 01:16:45.790
material than wood, which
makes a lot of sense.

01:16:45.790 --> 01:16:50.500
But interestingly, you can see
that bone is maybe five or four

01:16:50.500 --> 01:16:51.940
times stiffer than any.

01:16:51.940 --> 01:16:55.570
So bone is an extremely
stiff material Furthermore,

01:16:55.570 --> 01:16:57.330
in terms of the
modulus of rupture,

01:16:57.330 --> 01:16:59.980
you can see that wood and
concrete aren't that different,

01:16:59.980 --> 01:17:01.660
but the bone is enormously high.

01:17:01.660 --> 01:17:04.000
That means it takes
much more stress

01:17:04.000 --> 01:17:06.308
to be able to break
a piece of bone.

01:17:06.308 --> 01:17:07.725
So now these are
some of the clues

01:17:07.725 --> 01:17:11.690
that we're beginning to see,
as to why it's so difficult,

01:17:11.690 --> 01:17:16.917
why it's so easy to break
materials with hands and feet.

01:17:16.917 --> 01:17:19.250
And now here's some of the
critical breaking parameters.

01:17:19.250 --> 01:17:22.040
I don't want to go into
them in great detail.

01:17:22.040 --> 01:17:26.260
But let me just go to some
interesting numbers here.

01:17:26.260 --> 01:17:28.570
First of all, to
break a piece of wood

01:17:28.570 --> 01:17:33.150
requires kinetic energy
according to this model

01:17:33.150 --> 01:17:34.550
of about 6 joules.

01:17:34.550 --> 01:17:37.970
And remember, we estimated that
a good, expert karate strike,

01:17:37.970 --> 01:17:41.050
there might be something
like 50 joules available,

01:17:41.050 --> 01:17:42.650
so there's plenty
of energy there.

01:17:42.650 --> 01:17:45.010
And notice that
according to this model,

01:17:45.010 --> 01:17:48.220
it takes about 9 joules to break
that piece of concrete, which

01:17:48.220 --> 01:17:51.860
is still much less
than we have available,

01:17:51.860 --> 01:17:53.720
so it should be doable.

01:17:53.720 --> 01:17:57.190
I also want to point out
that this model is really not

01:17:57.190 --> 01:17:57.940
terribly accurate.

01:17:57.940 --> 01:17:59.550
And if you read
your handout, you'll

01:17:59.550 --> 01:18:01.830
find a more accurate model.

01:18:01.830 --> 01:18:03.670
And in particular, the
thing which is worse

01:18:03.670 --> 01:18:06.160
is about this model
is that it predicts

01:18:06.160 --> 01:18:11.690
that the velocities available
to be able to break wood

01:18:11.690 --> 01:18:13.370
and concrete are very close.

01:18:13.370 --> 01:18:16.720
It turns out that with a
more careful calculation

01:18:16.720 --> 01:18:18.970
that this is about right.

01:18:18.970 --> 01:18:22.180
It takes about 4 meters
per second to break wood,

01:18:22.180 --> 01:18:24.370
which means that even
a beginner can easily

01:18:24.370 --> 01:18:25.810
break a piece of wood.

01:18:25.810 --> 01:18:27.460
But it actually
takes more like 8

01:18:27.460 --> 01:18:30.850
or 9 meters per second
of initial velocity

01:18:30.850 --> 01:18:33.850
to be able to break
a piece of concrete.

01:18:33.850 --> 01:18:39.700
So that concludes the
theoretical part of this.

01:18:39.700 --> 01:18:41.920
And now let's do some
experiments to actually

01:18:41.920 --> 01:18:43.960
be able to demonstrate this.

01:18:43.960 --> 01:18:49.070
And I would first like to begin
by demonstrating a hammer fist

01:18:49.070 --> 01:18:53.920
strike of this pile
of boards over here.

01:18:53.920 --> 01:18:57.730
And let me begin by
again emphasizing

01:18:57.730 --> 01:19:01.260
that breaking is not really
an inherent part of karate.

01:19:01.260 --> 01:19:04.390
It's not usually practiced
in practice session.

01:19:04.390 --> 01:19:06.320
It's really not important.

01:19:06.320 --> 01:19:08.710
It's very showy, but the
only reason that we do it

01:19:08.710 --> 01:19:10.510
is because we want
to be able to gauge

01:19:10.510 --> 01:19:12.700
the effectiveness
of the techniques,

01:19:12.700 --> 01:19:15.400
and also because it demonstrates
a very nice piece of physics

01:19:15.400 --> 01:19:18.560
from our point of view.

01:19:18.560 --> 01:19:20.080
The other thing
is that these are

01:19:20.080 --> 01:19:21.550
rather difficult techniques.

01:19:21.550 --> 01:19:24.970
And so I would really like
to ask for complete silence

01:19:24.970 --> 01:19:27.850
during the demonstration
that we're going to give you.

01:19:27.850 --> 01:19:29.350
So this is going
to be a hammer fist

01:19:29.350 --> 01:19:31.230
strike of a pile of boards.

01:19:48.550 --> 01:19:49.050
Hyah!

01:19:54.000 --> 01:19:56.970
[APPLAUSE]

01:20:05.440 --> 01:20:09.990
Now I'd like to demonstrate
some more intricate techniques.

01:20:09.990 --> 01:20:12.740
Johnathan is now going to
demonstrate a knife hand

01:20:12.740 --> 01:20:15.090
strike and a Yoko
Gari, which he's

01:20:15.090 --> 01:20:17.110
going to break some
materials with his hand

01:20:17.110 --> 01:20:18.120
and then with his fist.

01:20:18.120 --> 01:20:18.620
John.

01:21:03.718 --> 01:21:06.208
FELD: [VOCALIZING]

01:21:06.208 --> 01:21:08.200
[VOCALIZING]

01:21:08.200 --> 01:21:11.188
[APPLAUSE]

01:21:20.250 --> 01:21:22.745
FELD: Now David is going
to demonstrate first

01:21:22.745 --> 01:21:24.620
breaking some boards
with a wheel kick, which

01:21:24.620 --> 01:21:27.900
is a very powerful technique
with the heel of his foot,

01:21:27.900 --> 01:21:30.150
and then some of
these concrete patio

01:21:30.150 --> 01:21:32.700
blocks afterwards with
a hammer fist strike.

01:22:04.968 --> 01:22:05.468
FELD: Hyah!

01:22:10.924 --> 01:22:11.916
Hyah!

01:22:11.916 --> 01:22:13.404
Hyah!

01:22:13.404 --> 01:22:16.876
[APPLAUSE]

01:22:24.316 --> 01:22:26.130
FELD: Now, another
part of karate,

01:22:26.130 --> 01:22:29.300
which is part of the
practice is fighting,

01:22:29.300 --> 01:22:31.340
sparring, or [INAUDIBLE].

01:22:31.340 --> 01:22:34.100
And we'd like to just give you
a little demonstration of that

01:22:34.100 --> 01:22:37.020
now, just to give you a
feeling for how it works.

01:22:43.570 --> 01:22:48.970
[INAUDIBLE] set.

01:22:48.970 --> 01:22:49.560
Hashime.

01:22:49.560 --> 01:22:51.400
FELD: [VOCALIZING]

01:23:05.694 --> 01:23:07.668
FELD: [INAUDIBLE]

01:23:07.668 --> 01:23:10.602
[APPLAUSE]

01:23:15.981 --> 01:23:17.427
Can I have the
next slide, please.

01:23:25.379 --> 01:23:30.720
I'd love to show you some still
photographs of the strobe movie

01:23:30.720 --> 01:23:34.080
we took some time ago to
study in more detail what

01:23:34.080 --> 01:23:36.950
happens when a fist breaks
a concrete patio block.

01:23:36.950 --> 01:23:38.700
You can see the concrete
patio block here.

01:23:38.700 --> 01:23:43.110
These photos were actually taken
in Doc Edgerton's strobe lab.

01:23:43.110 --> 01:23:46.130
And here, you can see that
the fist has four [INAUDIBLE]

01:23:46.130 --> 01:23:49.560
on it, so that we can
study the kinetic motion

01:23:49.560 --> 01:23:51.720
of different parts of the fist.

01:23:51.720 --> 01:23:53.550
And each of these frames--

01:23:53.550 --> 01:23:56.500
one, two, three,
four, five, six--

01:23:56.500 --> 01:23:59.720
are taken one millisecond apart.

01:23:59.720 --> 01:24:03.362
And what you can see here is--

01:24:03.362 --> 01:24:05.090
I'm very sorry I don't
have the movie up,

01:24:05.090 --> 01:24:06.690
because the movie
is really much more

01:24:06.690 --> 01:24:08.760
dramatic than these stills.

01:24:08.760 --> 01:24:11.070
But what you see
here is that the fist

01:24:11.070 --> 01:24:16.830
is in contact with the
block for 5 milliseconds.

01:24:16.830 --> 01:24:18.630
Then you can sort
of see by this point

01:24:18.630 --> 01:24:21.623
here that there is an enormous
deformation of the fist.

01:24:21.623 --> 01:24:23.040
And in the movie,
you can actually

01:24:23.040 --> 01:24:26.700
see that the fist really
doesn't behave like a bone.

01:24:26.700 --> 01:24:30.350
It really behaves more
like a blob of putty,

01:24:30.350 --> 01:24:33.450
and it completely distorts.

01:24:33.450 --> 01:24:35.040
And then as the
strike occurs, you

01:24:35.040 --> 01:24:39.210
can see this wave of muscle
pulse go right up the arm.

01:24:39.210 --> 01:24:41.363
It's really quite dramatic.

01:24:41.363 --> 01:24:42.780
The other interesting
thing is you

01:24:42.780 --> 01:24:46.800
can see that the fist was in
contact with the concrete here,

01:24:46.800 --> 01:24:50.190
but it's only 4
milliseconds later

01:24:50.190 --> 01:24:52.530
that the crack actually begins.

01:24:52.530 --> 01:24:54.030
And also, you can
see that the block

01:24:54.030 --> 01:24:57.340
cracks from the bottom up.

01:24:57.340 --> 01:25:00.630
And the reason for that
is because most materials

01:25:00.630 --> 01:25:04.380
are stronger under compression
than under tension.

01:25:04.380 --> 01:25:07.290
And so what happens is that
the concrete breaks here

01:25:07.290 --> 01:25:08.540
rather than there.

01:25:08.540 --> 01:25:11.040
May I have the
next slide, please?

01:25:11.040 --> 01:25:12.510
Now one of those
dots, I don't know

01:25:12.510 --> 01:25:17.030
remember what dot it was,
we marked the displacement

01:25:17.030 --> 01:25:21.550
as a function of time out of
all of these different-- of each

01:25:21.550 --> 01:25:23.140
of these dots.

01:25:23.140 --> 01:25:25.440
And from there, we can
take the derivative.

01:25:25.440 --> 01:25:28.220
We can study the rate
of change of that

01:25:28.220 --> 01:25:31.853
and get an idea of the
velocity of the hand.

01:25:31.853 --> 01:25:33.270
And you can see
here that the hand

01:25:33.270 --> 01:25:36.390
started at a velocity
of 12 meters per second.

01:25:36.390 --> 01:25:38.980
And it gradually
decelerated to 0.

01:25:38.980 --> 01:25:41.650
And you can actually study
the acceleration here

01:25:41.650 --> 01:25:43.710
by simply taking the
derivative of that.

01:25:43.710 --> 01:25:45.830
And you're experts in that.

01:25:45.830 --> 01:25:49.230
And you can see that the
amount of deceleration which

01:25:49.230 --> 01:25:51.720
occurs here is quite enormous--

01:25:51.720 --> 01:25:55.250
around 3,000 meters per second
squared, which is really

01:25:55.250 --> 01:25:59.310
an enormous stress that
the hand must undergo

01:25:59.310 --> 01:26:02.120
when decelerating so rapidly.

01:26:02.120 --> 01:26:03.740
May I have the
next slide, please.

01:26:06.820 --> 01:26:09.870
I didn't fully break the
stack of boards today.

01:26:09.870 --> 01:26:13.500
And in this picture, I also
didn't break a stack of boards.

01:26:13.500 --> 01:26:15.120
And probably the
reason is the same.

01:26:15.120 --> 01:26:17.100
Here you can see
what happens when

01:26:17.100 --> 01:26:20.580
you hit the boards not
in the exact center,

01:26:20.580 --> 01:26:22.265
but off to the side slightly.

01:26:22.265 --> 01:26:23.640
And you can see
that what happens

01:26:23.640 --> 01:26:30.228
is a kind of wave of destruction
of broken boards occurs.

01:26:30.228 --> 01:26:32.520
And you can see it's trying
to move towards the center.

01:26:32.520 --> 01:26:35.150
But you can see that the bottom
one didn't actually break.

01:26:35.150 --> 01:26:37.650
And there's another interesting
thing that you can see here,

01:26:37.650 --> 01:26:41.070
and that is that even though
the hand has only moved two

01:26:41.070 --> 01:26:44.280
or three boards down--
that's 2 or 3 inches--

01:26:44.280 --> 01:26:47.310
you can see that the boards are
broken well, well below that,

01:26:47.310 --> 01:26:49.877
maybe 8 or 10 inches below.

01:26:49.877 --> 01:26:51.960
And that shows that actually
what's happening here

01:26:51.960 --> 01:26:53.418
is that the first
board is breaking

01:26:53.418 --> 01:26:57.410
the second, and the second the
third, and so forth and so on.

01:26:57.410 --> 01:26:59.780
And then you can see the same
thing occurs in concrete.

01:26:59.780 --> 01:27:04.030
And here again, these blocks
were not hit in the center.

01:27:04.030 --> 01:27:06.850
But it hit off center, and
that produced a situation

01:27:06.850 --> 01:27:09.780
where the crack
propagated inward,

01:27:09.780 --> 01:27:13.230
but then it was not possible to
break both boards [INAUDIBLE]..

01:27:13.230 --> 01:27:16.055
Can I have the
next slide, please.

01:27:16.055 --> 01:27:17.680
And finally, I'm not
going to have time

01:27:17.680 --> 01:27:19.970
to go into all the
details to this,

01:27:19.970 --> 01:27:23.740
but I just want to say that, in
addition to the rather enormous

01:27:23.740 --> 01:27:27.420
differences in the
elastic properties of bone

01:27:27.420 --> 01:27:29.590
and the rupture
properties of bone

01:27:29.590 --> 01:27:33.140
compared to those of
building materials,

01:27:33.140 --> 01:27:35.600
there is also a
fact of positioning,

01:27:35.600 --> 01:27:38.500
which is very, very important,
the geometrical factor.

01:27:38.500 --> 01:27:40.720
And this is very
intuitive, of course.

01:27:40.720 --> 01:27:43.690
If you hit this
target flat-footed,

01:27:43.690 --> 01:27:47.080
you're likely to hurt yourself
a lot more than if you

01:27:47.080 --> 01:27:48.970
hit with a knife foot.

01:27:48.970 --> 01:27:52.290
And you can actually include
the geometrical effects

01:27:52.290 --> 01:27:58.060
in the analysis along
with the bulk properties,

01:27:58.060 --> 01:28:01.170
the Young's modulus and
the modulus of rupture.

01:28:01.170 --> 01:28:04.300
And if you do that, if you
look at the very, very end

01:28:04.300 --> 01:28:06.550
of the handout that
we have, you will

01:28:06.550 --> 01:28:11.920
see that in fact, a properly
positioned foot like this,

01:28:11.920 --> 01:28:15.580
actually, the force
necessary to break this bone

01:28:15.580 --> 01:28:19.120
is about 2,000 times
greater than the force

01:28:19.120 --> 01:28:21.320
necessary to break
the concrete block.

01:28:21.320 --> 01:28:25.310
You can see it's an
enormous differential.

01:28:25.310 --> 01:28:31.190
So now, I would like to--

01:28:31.190 --> 01:28:32.830
first of all, in
conclusion, I'd like

01:28:32.830 --> 01:28:35.720
to thank my colleagues
for participating

01:28:35.720 --> 01:28:36.800
in this demonstration.

01:28:36.800 --> 01:28:39.850
[APPLAUSE]

